  "body": "### What problem are you trying to solve?\n\nThere is currently no native way in HTML to allow users to select a range of values (e.g. a minimum and maximum) using a single input element. The existing <input type=\"range\"> only supports one value.\n\nThis is a common pattern in modern interfaces (e.g., price range filters), and developers are forced to either:\n\nUse two <input type=\"range\"> elements manually synchronized, which causes UX and accessibility issues, or\n\nInclude third-party libraries that simulate this behavior with JavaScript.\n\nThis results in unnecessary complexity and inconsistent behavior across applications.\n\n### What solutions exist today?\n\nUse two separate <input type=\"range\"> elements (with event listeners to coordinate values and layout).\n\nUse a JavaScript library which:\n -Adds dependencies,\n -Requires manual setup for accessibility,\n -Often overrides native behavior and styling.\n\nNone of these are optimal for simple, accessible, semantic web forms.\n\n### How would you solve it?\n\nIntroduce a new input variation or extend the current type=\"range\" input to support two thumbs (start and end values). Possible syntax examples:\n\n`<input type=\"range\" dual min=\"0\" max=\"100\" value=\"20,80\">\n//or\n<input type=\"range-range\" min=\"0\" max=\"100\" value-start=\"20\" value-end=\"80\">\n`\n\n### Anything else?\n\n- This feature would significantly reduce the need for JS workarounds and improve semantic form design.\n- Similar components are already native in other platforms (e.g., Android's RangeSlider or iOS UIKit).\n- A native implementation ensures consistency, better accessibility, and smaller codebases.\n\nThank you for considering this suggestion!",
